// A component (`len`) shared by two DIFFERENT archetypes (A, B) must be written per-archetype: a map that
// matches only A (via A's unique marker `mark_a`) and writes `len` must update A's row only, leaving B's row
// untouched. Regression (BUG — this test is RED): the write to the shared component BROADCASTS to every
// archetype that owns it, so writing A's `len` also clobbered B's `len` (B prints 5 instead of its seeded 9).
// This is the defect that forces per-archetype duplicate column names — it makes the shared-vocabulary ECS
// style (one `len`/`pos`/`color` reused across archetypes) silently corrupt sibling rows, and a shared loop
// counter written+read in a map spins forever. Expected once fixed: A=5 (written), B=9 (untouched).
// RUN: %arche run %s | grep -qx "A=5"
// RUN: %arche run %s | grep -qx "B=9"
#import { fmt }

len    :: int;
mark_a :: int;
mark_b :: int;

A :: arche { mark_a, len }
B :: arche { mark_b, len }
[1]A ?abort;
[1]B ?abort;

seed :: system eff {
  insert(A { mark_a: 0, len: 0 })(_:, _ok:);
  insert(B { mark_b: 0, len: 9 })(_:, _ok:);
}

set_a :: system eff { map (query { mark_a, len })(len) eff { len = 5; }; }
show_a :: system eff { map (query { mark_a, len }) eff { fmt.printf("A=%d\n", len); }; }
show_b :: system eff { map (query { mark_b, len }) eff { fmt.printf("B=%d\n", len); }; }

#run seq({ seed, set_a, show_a, show_b })
